---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import DevFormatJson from '@/components/tools/dev/DevFormatJson.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="json-minify">
  <DevFormatJson client:load mode="minify" />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>What minifying JSON removes</h2>
    <p>
      To minify JSON, the tool removes only insignificant whitespace: the spaces, tabs and newlines
      between tokens that a pretty printer added. Strings, key order and numbers stay the same. The tool
      parses the document and writes the parsed value again, so the output is always valid JSON with
      exactly the same data. An invalid input gives an error, not a damaged string.
    </p>

    <h2>Does minifying make it faster?</h2>
    <p>
      Minification saves many raw bytes and very few transferred bytes. Indentation is the most
      compressible part of a text file: long runs of identical characters in predictable positions,
      which gzip and Brotli remove almost for free. A document that is 28% smaller after minification is
      usually only 3&ndash;6% smaller after Brotli.
    </p>
    <p>So minify when the limit is on uncompressed bytes:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A database column with a length cap.</li>
      <li>A message-queue or webhook payload size limit (SQS 256 KB, EventBridge 256 KB, Lambda 6 MB synchronous).</li>
      <li>A cookie, a URL query parameter, or a header value.</li>
      <li>localStorage, which is quota-limited per origin and stores strings uncompressed.</li>
      <li>An embedded device with a fixed receive buffer.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Do not minify a static <code>.json</code> file served over HTTP only for speed. Enable compression
      on the server and keep the readable version.
    </p>

    <h2>Sorting keys before minifying</h2>
    <p>
      Object member order has no meaning in JSON, but a byte-level comparison (a checksum, a cache key,
      a text diff) treats a reordered document as a different one. If you hash JSON to detect change, or
      compare two API responses written by different runtime versions, turn on
      <strong>Sort keys A→Z</strong> first. Array order has meaning, and the tool never changes it.
    </p>

    <h2>Numbers survive, formatting does not</h2>
    <p>
      If you will compare the minified document with the original, note that the round trip normalises
      number formatting. <code>1.50</code> becomes <code>1.5</code>, <code>1e3</code> becomes
      <code>1000</code>, and <code>-0</code> becomes <code>0</code>. The values are identical, but the
      text is not. If you need byte-identical numeric formatting, for example for a signature over a
      canonical form, do not send it through a parser.
    </p>

    <h2>Minifying very large files</h2>
    <p>
      The tool parses the whole document in memory, so the limit is your device, not an upload limit.
      Tens of megabytes are fine on a desktop browser. A phone has problems sooner. For files over a
      megabyte, use the file button: pasting a large document into a textarea is slower than reading
      the file.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
